<p style="max-width:72rem">This sandbox runs the RedeemID plugin against a real Shopify store. The <a href="/">Harborview Ledger</a> is a faux news site with free and paywalled stories, and its test pages each try one piece of the plugin. Switch to a test account on the Test accounts tab, then check what you see on the <a href="/account/">Account</a> page (its Visitor state panel shows the raw state) and against the tables below.</p>

<h2>Features</h2>

<p style="max-width:72rem">Features are site behaviours a membership unlocks, from the plugin’s feature registry. The theme registers three and only places the plugin’s blocks. <strong>Ad-free reading</strong> goes to Gold, or to anyone with the <code>ad-free-addon</code> tag, which stacks with any tier. It is mapped on RedeemID → Access → Features, and it hides the sponsor slot on stories and the home page. <strong>Early briefing</strong> goes to Silver or Gold, is set in code (so that screen shows it as managed in code), and shows tomorrow’s briefing on <a href="/briefings/">Briefings</a>. <strong>Supporter</strong> goes to anyone with the <code>supporter</code> tag (set in code): only Sam Supporter among the test accounts, and the contribution form thanks them instead of asking. All three appear as chips in the header and on the <a href="/account/">Account</a> page.</p>

<table class="widefat striped" style="max-width:72rem"><thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>Ad-free reading</th><th>Early briefing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Guest</td><td>No: sponsor slot</td><td>No: prompt</td></tr><tr><td>Riley Reader</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Milo Member</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Sasha Silver</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, from Silver</td></tr><tr><td>Gia Gold</td><td>Yes, from Gold</td><td>Yes, from Gold</td></tr><tr><td>Paz Premium</td><td>Yes, from Gold</td><td>Yes, from Silver and Gold</td></tr><tr><td>Ada Add-on</td><td>Yes, from Ad-free add-on</td><td>Yes, from Silver</td></tr><tr><td>Lou Lapsed</td><td>No (disabled)</td><td>No (disabled)</td></tr><tr><td>Stella Stale</td><td>Yes, from Gold</td><td>Yes, from Gold</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class="description" style="max-width:72rem">The plugin decides each feature on the server, so a withheld block is not in the page source at all. The early briefing carries the marker <code>LEDGER-FEATURE-EARLY-BRIEFING</code>, which must appear only for Silver and Gold. Ad-free holders also get the <code>redeemid-feature-ad-free</code> body class (the masthead says “Ad-free”) and <code>window.redeemidFeatures</code>, which is presentation only.</p>

<h2>Content types and their rules</h2>

<p style="max-width:72rem">The plugin gates more than posts. The theme registers four demo post types and four taxonomies (Editions, Series, Neighborhoods and a Topics taxonomy shared by posts and every type) and seeds rules on each layer: RedeemID → Access → Content rules for the post types, each term’s edit screen for term rules, and the Access box on each item. The table shows which layer should decide each item and the badge a guest should see.</p>

<table class="widefat striped" style="max-width:72rem"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Type</th><th>Decided by</th><th>Guest badge</th></tr></thead><tbody><?php foreach ( redeemid_demo_cpt_items() as $ledger_key => $ledger_item ) : ?><?php $ledger_type = get_post_type_object( $ledger_item['type'] ); ?><?php $ledger_base = 'post' === $ledger_item['type'] ? '' : (string) ( $ledger_type->rewrite['slug'] ?? '' ); ?><tr><td><a href="<?php echo esc_url( '/' . ( $ledger_base ? $ledger_base . '/' : '' ) . $ledger_key . '/' ); ?>"><?php echo esc_html( $ledger_item['title'] ); ?></a></td><td><?php echo esc_html( $ledger_type ? $ledger_type->labels->singular_name : $ledger_item['type'] ); ?></td><td><?php echo esc_html( $ledger_item['rule'] ); ?></td><td><?php echo esc_html( $ledger_item['expect'] ); ?></td></tr><?php endforeach; ?></tbody></table>
<p class="description" style="max-width:72rem">Dates count from the last seeding or restore: the storm briefing locks after 7 days, the Tidewater report opens after 14 and the Weekend edition’s Opens rule ends after 30. Restore test content starts them again. Locked items carry a <code>LEDGER-SECRET-CPT-…</code> marker; items that should be open to everyone carry <code>LEDGER-OPEN-CPT-…</code>.</p>

<h2>What to try</h2>

<ol style="max-width:72rem">
	<li><strong>Sign in.</strong> Use “Sign in” in the header. Shopify’s sign-in page should open in the site’s language and send you to <a href="/auth-callback/">/auth-callback/</a> and then back to the page you started on. The Visitor state panel on the <a href="/account/">Account</a> page should say you are signed in, linked, and (for a new customer) a shadow user.</li>
	<li><strong>Check every control.</strong> <a href="/sign-in-controls/">Sign-in controls</a> shows the block in each mode. The page HTML is the same for everyone; only the hydrated control changes.</li>
	<li><strong>Open the members area.</strong> <a href="/members-area/">Members area</a> releases its content to any signed-in visitor. As a guest you should see a message and a sign-in button.</li>
	<li><strong>Test tag rules.</strong> <a href="/tag-gates/">Tag gates</a> has gates for <code>gold-member</code>, <code>gold-member</code> + <code>silver-member</code>, a teaser fallback, a tag nobody has, and an open control. Add or remove the tags on your customer in Shopify (or buy a mapped product, see <a href="/join/">Become a member</a>) and reload.</li>
	<li><strong>Try to leak the paywalled post.</strong> As a guest, open <a href="/members-only-article/">the members article</a>, view its source, its <a href="/feed/">RSS</a>, its <a href="/wp-json/wp/v2/posts?slug=members-only-article">REST response</a> and <a href="/?s=LEDGER-SECRET">a search for the secret phrase</a>. The phrase <code>LEDGER-SECRET-MEMBERS-ONLY</code> must not appear anywhere unless you are a <code>gold-member</code>.</li>
	<li><strong>Read the Ledger as each account.</strong> Switch to each test account and open one story of each badge from the <a href="/">home page</a>. The result should match the Test accounts tab. Paywalled stories carry a <code>LEDGER-SECRET-NEWS-…</code> leak marker that must stay out of the source, feed, REST and search for anyone not entitled.</li>
	<li><strong>Try the meter.</strong> RedeemID → Access → Content rules has the meter on: a signed-in reader without access opens 2 locked stories a month free. Switch to <strong>Mia Meter</strong>, who has 1 left, and open a Gold story from the <a href="/">home page</a>: it opens with “Free story 2 of 2 this month” and reads free again all month. Open another Gold story: it shows the wall with “You've read your 2 free stories this month.” As a guest, the wall asks you to sign in for the free stories instead. The other test accounts start each month with the meter used up, so the Test accounts tab still holds; <strong>Save and reset test accounts</strong> puts Mia back to 1 left.</li>
	<li><strong>Check the section rules.</strong> The Investigations stories have no gate block: the plugin's content rules lock them (RedeemID → Access → Content rules, and each category's edit screen). <em>Harbor pilots</em> opens for Gold, <em>The arts council’s grant audit</em> for Gold + Silver (it is in Investigations and Culture), and <em>Ferry fuel contract</em> is open to everyone while its "Free to read" tag lasts. The post list's Access column says where each story’s rule comes from. Change a category’s rule and reload a story: the change applies at once.</li>
	<li><strong>Check the content types.</strong> Open each item in the table above as a guest and as each test account. Then try to leak them: <a href="/?s=LEDGER-SECRET-CPT">search</a>, the <a href="/reports/feed/">Reports feed</a> and the REST routes (<a href="/wp-json/wp/v2/ledger_report">reports</a>, <a href="/wp-json/wp/v2/ledger_briefing">briefings</a>, <a href="/wp-json/wp/v2/ledger_guide">guides</a>). Change a post type row, a term rule or an item’s Access box and reload: the badge and the paywall follow at once. Corrections never lock, because gating is switched off for that post type.</li>
	<li><strong>Refresh membership.</strong> Signed in and linked, use “Refresh membership” in the header (or in a story’s paywall box). It calls the plugin’s <code>POST /redeemid/v1/account/sync</code> and reloads; a second press within a minute says to wait. For a simulated test account it puts the seeded state back; for a real customer it asks Shopify, so a tag you just added in Shopify opens the matching stories.</li>
	<li><strong>Handle a privacy request.</strong> Signed in as a test account, use Download my data or Delete my data on the <a href="/account/">Account</a> page, then confirm from the email. As an administrator, open Tools → Export Personal Data and Tools → Erase Personal Data: each request has a Shopify column with the customer and where the request came from. Export lists the synced fields, the activity log and the webhooks for that customer; Erase removes them and keeps the WordPress account. “Also request erasure in Shopify” is refused for the simulated accounts, which don’t exist in Shopify; for an account pointed at a real dev-store customer it asks Shopify, which erases after its waiting period and then sends <code>customers/redact</code>.</li>
	<li><strong>Check the features.</strong> Switch to each account and compare the header chips, a story’s sponsor slot and the <a href="/briefings/">Briefings</a> page with the Features table. As Ada Add-on, remove <code>ad-free-addon</code> on RedeemID → Customers (or let a refund do it): the sponsor slot comes back on the next page, and she keeps Early briefing from Silver. RedeemID → Access → Test prints each account’s features with the route that grants them, and RedeemID → Access → Features lets you remap Ad-free reading.</li>
	<li><strong>Contribute.</strong> <a href="/support/">Support the Ledger</a>, and the end of every story, has a pay-what-you-want form. Pick a preset or type an amount: Shopify checkout opens with that many of the $1 Contribution product (handle <code>contribution</code>). Close it without paying. Switch to Sam Supporter: the form thanks Sam instead of asking. For a real customer, build a Flow workflow on <strong>Order paid</strong> that adds the <code>supporter</code> tag when a line item's product has the tag <code>contribution</code>, buy on a test payment, and refresh membership.</li>
	<li><strong>Redirect mode.</strong> <a href="/gate-redirect/">Redirect gate</a> sends non-members to the Become a member page.</li>
	<li><strong>Link an existing account.</strong> Sign in with a plain WordPress (non-admin) user whose email matches your Shopify customer, then use “Confirm your email” on the <a href="/account/">Account</a> page: the sign-in page opens with that email filled in, and after the code the account is linked with no extra step. Administrators are refused on purpose.</li>
	<li><strong>Come back as an existing reader.</strong> Signed out, use “Sign in” with the email of a plain WordPress (non-admin) user that isn’t linked yet. After the code you should be signed in to that existing account, already linked, with no password asked.</li>
	<li><strong>Sign out.</strong> “Sign out” should end both the WordPress and the Shopify session. Signing in again should ask Shopify for a code.</li>
	<li><strong>Admin side.</strong> In the <a href="/wp-admin/admin.php?page=redeemid">RedeemID</a> admin menu: setup, customers, access (with Test, which shows the gate decision for any user), the webhook and app event logs, and debugging.</li>
</ol>

<p class="description">Not in the plugin yet, so not testable here: the account card, the full member menu (today the header shows only the feature chips) and a cart count. Catalog blocks come from Shopify’s own plugin, not RedeemID.</p>
